# Plain-English changelog to Slack (auto-posted)

A GitHub Action already posts every change to the Infinity Windows Slack
changelog automatically — once when a PR is **opened** ("Proposed change, not
yet merged") and again when it **merges to master** ("Shipped to master"). You
do **not** post to Slack yourself, and you must **not** use the Slack MCP for
this. There is nothing to send by hand.

Because the Action can only see the **PR title** and **PR body**, your job is
simply to write both in very plain, human, non-technical English so the
auto-post reads well for a non-technical reader (crew & office — Taylor &
Ammon).

## Write it like a person, not a changelog bot

The reader is **non-technical**. Describe, in everyday language, **what changed
and why it matters to crew/office**, and **whether it's live**. Do **not**
mention file names, functions, or technical terms unless there is truly no plain
way to say it.

- **PR title:** a short, plain-English "what changed for users" (not
  commit-speak). e.g. "Keep installers signed in for the whole workday".
- **PR body:** start with 1–3 plain sentences on what changed, why it helps, and
  whether it's live, before any technical detail. The Action posts the title
  plus the first part of the body, so the human summary must come first.

GOOD:

```
Making installer sign-in faster

Right now crew have to re-enter their PIN too often. This change keeps them
signed in for the whole workday so they can get to their jobs quicker. Not live
yet — this is under review.
```

BAD (never write like this):

```
Refactored auth session persistence in App.tsx; bumped maxAge to 7d.
```

More guidance:
- Describe user-visible impact first; skip internal noise (lockfile bumps,
  formatting-only churn) unless that was the whole point of the task.
- Say plainly whether the change is live yet or still under review.
- Never include secrets, tokens, or credentials in the title or body.
